DEFERRED
The L1 overflow that D-C6 names is FIXED, and concat-32 prefill now runs end to end on this
mesh for the first time. What is deferred is the numerical claim behind it on Qwen at the two
shortest lengths.

FIXED, and qualified: `recipes.dense_matmul_output_blocks` (commit 60823a3888f, c-defects
attempt 1) re-blocks the 2D multicast matmul's output so its circular buffers stop growing with
the batch. `ttnn` defaults `out_block_h`/`out_block_w` to `per_core_M`/`per_core_N`, and the
concat-32 prefill multiplies the row count by 32, so the output circular buffer grew with the
whole per-core tile count. Modelled against silicon to the byte:
  concat-32 len128 QKV: resolved per_core=(32,14) in0_block_w=4 -> model 1 671 168 B,
                        validate_circular_buffer_region said 1 669 312 B
  concat-32 len256 QKV: resolved per_core=(64,14) in0_block_w=4 -> model 3 112 960 B,
                        validate_circular_buffer_region said 3 111 104 B
Every concat length from 128 to 2048 now resolves out_block=(16,14) at 950 272 B, under the
GALAXY_MATMUL_CB_BUDGET of 1 400 000 B, which sits between the largest currently-qualified
config (1 343 488 B, Llama's 2048-token `wo`) and the measured ceiling (1 499 136 B) - so every
shape Milestone B qualified keeps its program config byte for byte.

MEASURED on silicon, three fresh processes each (c-defects attempt 1, RESULTS.md):
  Llama 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048   PASS x3 each   concat-32 == sequential argmax in 32/32
  Qwen  512                          PASS x3
  Qwen  128                          FAIL x3       slots [4, 11] disagree
  Qwen  256                          FAIL x3       slot  [25]    disagrees
  Qwen  1024, 2048                   NOT RUN - `_BATCHED_LENGTHS` stops at 512 and its own
                                     comment says to extend only once 128-512 pass once.
All eleven of Milestone B's concat-32 runs, on both models, died on the L1 overflow before any
row's logits could be inspected, so every number above is new.

WHY DEFERRED, not fixed: the residual is not the overflow and not a tie. `logs/concat_tie_probe.log`
shows the concatenated and sequential paths' logits differing by up to 1.06 - eight bfloat16 ulp
at magnitude 15, not one - and 7 of 32 rows have a top-two gap below that, which is the size of
effect that predicts 2 disagreements at 128, 1 at 256 and 0 at 512. So concat-32 is close to
sequential prefill but NOT numerically equivalent, and the cause of a 1.06 divergence is not
established: it is consistent with a different accumulation order and also with a real
cross-row effect, and this job did not separate them.

WHAT MILESTONE C THEREFORE DOES NOT HAVE: batched (concat-32) prefill is not qualified on
Qwen. Llama's sweep passes at all five lengths, Qwen's passes only at 512, and Qwen at 1024 and
2048 has never been run. Nothing downstream may be built on concat-32 for Qwen. Milestone C's
prefill is sequential per row by the 2026-08-28 scope decision and this changes nothing about
that: sequential prefill is the accepted fallback and it is qualified on both models.

NOTHING WAS RELAXED. The gate is an exact argmax comparison and it still is; the failing cases
are reported as failing.

Logs: tttv2_milestone_c_evidence/defects/logs/ - `dc6_{q,l}_concat_len*_run{1,2,3}.log`,
`c12_q_concat_len128_run1.log` (the first run that ever placed), `concat_tie_probe.log`.

--- c-defects attempt 6, 2026-08-31: re-affirmed DEFERRED, and nothing was re-measured ---

This attempt did not re-open D-C6 and did not re-run the concat-32 sweep. The call above
stands as attempt 1 made it, with attempt 1's numbers behind it.

What Milestone C therefore does not have, stated once more because the brief requires the
handoff to say it plainly:

  * batched (concat-32) prefill is NOT qualified on Qwen. Llama passes at 128, 256, 512,
    1024 and 2048, three fresh processes each; Qwen passes only at 512, fails 3/3 at 128
    (slots [4, 11]) and 3/3 at 256 (slot [25]), and has never been run at 1024 or 2048.
  * area 2's real question - do padded rows change an active row's logits at active batch
    16, 31 and 32 - is therefore answered for Llama and NOT for Qwen.
  * nothing downstream may be built on concat-32 for Qwen. Milestone C's prefill is
    sequential per row by the 2026-08-28 scope decision, sequential prefill is qualified on
    both models, and this changes nothing about that.

The residual is NOT the L1 overflow D-C6 names - that is fixed and concat-32 places on this
mesh - and it is NOT a bfloat16 tie: the concatenated and sequential logits differ by up to
1.06, eight ulp at magnitude 15 (logs/concat_tie_probe.log). Whether that is a different
accumulation order or a real cross-row effect is not established, and no attempt of this job
has separated them.

Attempt 6's weight-release change (299440bb276) touches teardown only and does not bear on
any of the above.

--- c-defects attempt 9, 2026-09-01: the residual is BOUNDED, and the "refuted" line is too narrow ---

The probe line quoted above - "every diverged slot is an exact tie: False" - is correct, and the
rest of the same table is what matters (logs/concat_tie_probe.log):

  slot 11:  seq gap=0.125  bat gap=0.125  differs=True   max|seq-bat| over the row = 0.75
  slot 13:  seq gap=0.375  bat gap=0      differs=True   max|seq-bat| over the row = 1.5
  diverged=[11, 13];  widest sequential top-two gap among diverged slots = 0.375
  max|seq-bat| across ALL 32 slots ranges 0.656 .. 2.281

  1. The two diverged slots are the two with the SMALLEST top-two gaps - one and three bfloat16
     ulps at magnitude 15. No wide-gap slot diverges.
  2. The two paths differ by 5..18 ulps everywhere. So the accumulation-order difference exceeds
     the top-two gap at exactly the diverging slots and is below it at the other thirty.
  3. The residual is therefore bounded and is NOT cross-row contamination: it is argmax being a
     discontinuous function of a quantity the two paths agree on only to ~1-2 logit units.
     Llama's 480 slot-comparisons (32 slots x 5 lengths x 3 processes) never meet a gap that
     small, which is why the same shared recipe passes there.

NOTHING WAS RELAXED. The claim compares argmax, it fails 3/3 at 128 and 3/3 at 256, and that is
the recorded result. What a fix would have to be is now on record: either a tighter-than-bfloat16
accumulation in the concat-32 reduction, or a discriminator that bounds the logit difference
rather than matching an argmax - and the second weakens the claim, so it belongs to whoever owns
the gate wording, not to this job.

STILL DEFERRED.

--- c-defects attempt 10, 2026-09-01: area 2's real question is ASKED, and on Qwen it PASSES ---

STILL DEFERRED. The numerical claim above is unchanged and nothing here re-opens it. What
changes is a claim this file made about a DIFFERENT question, which was never measured.

THE CORRECTION. The attempt-6 block above says:

    area 2's real question - do padded rows change an active row's logits at active batch
    16, 31 and 32 - is therefore answered for Llama and NOT for Qwen.

That is wrong, and it was wrong when written. It conflates two different tests. The length
sweep (`test_<model>_concat32_matches_sequential_prefill_at_each_length`) compares concat-32
against SEQUENTIAL prefill; the padding question is a different node,
`test_<model>_concat32_padded_rows_change_no_active_rows_logits`, which holds the active rows
fixed and changes ONLY the filler rows. Before 2026-09-01 that node had ZERO runs in
RESULTS.md on EITHER model - Milestone B's six attempts (a3_{q,l}_concat_active{16,31,32}) all
died inside validate_circular_buffer_region before a single row's logits could be read. So the
question was answered for NEITHER model, and Llama's passing length sweep is not evidence
about it.

MEASURED, QWEN, three fresh processes, all three active levels, for the first time:
  zp1_qwen_concat_padded_rows_r1   3 passed   590.75 s   commit c4605147cb94
  zp2_qwen_concat_padded_rows_r2   3 passed   730.40 s   commit c27f187e5596
  zp3_qwen_concat_padded_rows_r3   3 passed   508.97 s   commit c27f187e5596
  params in each: active16, active31, active32
  clash lines 0, TT_FATAL/TT_THROW 0, SKIPPED 0, validate_circular_buffer_region 0 - in all
  three logs. HF_HOME=/localdev/ctr-apbernal/hf_data.
  (Both commits' production code is byte-identical to HEAD; c27f187e5596 is evidence-only.)

  => On Qwen, changing only the padding rows moves no active row's logits, at active batch 16
     and 31, bit-exactly (the test uses torch.equal, not a tolerance). active32 is a control:
     with 32 active rows there are no padding rows, so the two batches are identical.

  This also independently confirms the L1 overflow is gone: zero validate_circular_buffer_region
  lines in a node that could not survive that call at Milestone B.

LLAMA: zp4/zp5/zp6 are the same node on Llama and are IN FLIGHT at the time of writing. This
file will be wrong again if nobody records their outcome; see the attempt-10 handoff.

WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT CHANGE. It does not make concat-32 usable on Qwen: the length
sweep still fails 3/3 at 128 and 3/3 at 256, so the DEFERRED call stands and nothing downstream
may be built on concat-32 for Qwen. What it removes is a different worry - that padded rows
contaminate active rows - which is now measured on Qwen and is absent.
